findings.suggestion:
Replace all six `r.log.Error(...)` calls with `r.log.Debug(...)` to match the package-wide convention (Error on failure, Debug on success). If per-batch observability is desired on the error path, gate the Error log on `err != nil` and keep a Debug log for the success path, mirroring the sibling pattern:

```go
ids, err := r.fetchIDs(ctx, "annotation", cond)
if err != nil {
    return 0, err
}
r.log.Debug("Annotations to clean by time", "count", len(ids))

x, y := r.deleteByIDs(ctx, "annotation", ids)
if y != nil {
    r.log.Error("cleaned annotations by time", "count", len(ids), "affected", x, "err", y)
} else {
    r.log.Debug("cleaned annotations by time", "count", len(ids), "affected", x)
}
return x, y
```

Also consider dropping the `"ids", ids` field (or truncating it) to avoid logging potentially tens of thousands of IDs per batch.

Add a brief comment explaining the xorm `InsertMulti` ID behavior:

```go
// Preset IDs because xorm's InsertMulti does not write back auto-incremented IDs
// to slice elements (unlike single-row Insert). The preset value is included in
// the INSERT because innerInsertMulti skips auto-increment columns only when
// the field value is zero. This ensures a.ID matches the DB row's PK for FK linkage.
a := &annotations.Item{ID: int64(i + 1), ...}
```
